.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt PIE-WIFI 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pie-wifi
.Nd join WiFi networks and show the link
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl i Ar interface
.Cm scan
.Nm
.Op Fl i Ar interface
.Cm join
.Ar ssid
.Op Fl Fl open
.Nm
.Op Fl i Ar interface
.Cm forget
.Ar ssid
.Nm
.Cm list
.Nm
.Op Fl i Ar interface
.Cm status
.Nm
.Op Fl i Ar interface
.Cm country
.Ar code
.Nm
.Op Fl i Ar interface
.Cm load
.Nm
.Cm import
.Ar file
.Nm
.Op Fl i Ar interface
.Cm wait
.Ar seconds
.Nm
.Cm watch
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
stores the WiFi networks to join and gives them to the kernel.
The kernel's 802.11 stack joins the best of them in range and does the
WPA2 handshakes itself.
.Nm
talks to the interface through PIE80211Family's driver ioctls.
The interface is
.Ar interface .
Without
.Fl i ,
it is the first interface that answers PIE80211Family's status command.
.Pp
A network is open or WPA2-PSK with CCMP.
For a WPA2 network,
.Nm
stores the key that PBKDF2 derives from the passphrase and the SSID.
It never stores the passphrase.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm scan
Scan, wait four seconds, then list the networks in range, one per line.
A line holds the BSSID, the channel, the signal, the security and the
SSID, separated by tabs.
The scan is passive: the chip listens for the networks' beacons.
While the interface is joined to a network, it does not scan.
.Nm
then says so and lists the networks it saw before.
.It Cm join Ar ssid Op Fl Fl open
Store a network and give it to the kernel.
Without
.Fl Fl open ,
ask for its passphrase on the terminal, without echo.
A passphrase has 8 to 63 printable ASCII characters.
A stored network of the same SSID is replaced.
.It Cm forget Ar ssid
Remove a stored network, and take it from the kernel.
.It Cm list
List the stored networks, one per line, with the SSID, the security and
the priority, separated by tabs.
No key is shown.
The priority is 0: no command sets another, and the kernel does not use
it.
.It Cm status
Show the link: the 802.11 state, whether the link is up, and the last
problem with a network.
A WPA2 network that ends the association before its handshake is done,
as it does when the passphrase is wrong, is such a problem.
While associated, also show the SSID, the BSSID, the channel, the signal
and the rate.
.It Cm country Ar code
Store the regulatory country, two capital letters, and give it to the
kernel.
Without a stored country, none is given, and the chip keeps the default
of its firmware.
.It Cm load
Give the stored country and networks to the kernel, in place of the
networks it has.
.It Cm import Ar file
Store the networks of a setup file, then delete the file.
The file has an
.Li ssid=
line for each network.
A WPA2 network's line is followed by a
.Li passphrase=
line.
Empty lines and lines that start with
.Ql #
are skipped, and so is a UTF-8 byte order mark at the start.
Any other line, or a NUL byte, makes the import fail, and nothing is
stored.
The file may have at most 4096 bytes.
The networks reach the kernel with
.Cm load .
A file that cannot be deleted stays.
.Nm
says so after the count of the networks stored, and exits 3.
A file with the FAT read-only attribute has it cleared first, so that it
can be deleted.
.It Cm wait Ar seconds
Wait until the link is up, for at most
.Ar seconds ,
from 0 to 3600.
.It Cm watch
Give the stored country and networks to each WiFi interface when it
appears, as
.Cm load
does, and again when it comes back.
What fails is tried again once a minute, the country and the networks
each until the interface has them.
A failure is logged once, until it changes.
.Cm watch
runs until it is stopped, and logs to
.Xr syslog 3 .
rc starts it at boot.
A second
.Cm watch
exits at once.
.El
.Pp
An SSID is printed as it is, except that the backslash and any byte that
is not printable are printed as
.Li \e\&x
and two hex digits.
.Pp
.Cm status
and
.Cm wait
work for any user.
The other commands need root.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa /var/db/wifi/networks
The stored networks, one per line: the SSID in hex, the security, the key
in hex or
.Ql -
for an open network, and the priority.
The file has mode 0600, in a directory of mode 0700.
.It Pa /var/db/wifi/networks.lock
.Cm join ,
.Cm forget ,
.Cm import
and
.Cm country
hold an
.Xr flock 2
lock on it while they change the stored networks or the country, and
.Cm join ,
.Cm forget
and
.Cm country
until the kernel has the change.
.Cm load
and
.Cm watch
hold it while they read the stored country and the networks and give
them to the kernel.
.It Pa /var/db/wifi/country
The stored country.
.Cm country
replaces it through a temporary file, as the other commands replace the
networks file.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Cm wait
exits 1 when the time runs out before the link is up.
.Cm import
exits 3 when it stored the networks but the file stays.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Join a WPA2 network, then wait for the link:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# pie-wifi join 'Home Network'
Passphrase:
# pie-wifi wait 30 && pie-wifi status
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ifconfig 8
.Sh CAVEATS
At most 16 networks are stored.
.Pp
Deleting a setup file does not erase it from flash memory.
Its passphrases may stay readable on the card.
